# ==============================================================================
# pulsaros profile — base package list (Phase 2 of engine/build-iso.sh)
# One package per line, '#' comments ignored. This is what goes into the
# dnf --installroot base-cache bootstrap: a plain Fedora GNOME system,
# with no Pulsar OS / ParchaOS branding/apps yet (those come from
# PROFILE_REPO_PACKAGES in packages.sh, installed against the profile's
# own COPR in Phase 5). Keeping this list unbranded is what lets Phase
# 1's "get a plain Fedora GNOME live ISO building before any branding"
# checkpoint actually mean something — same pattern the KDE (pearos)
# profile used successfully.
#
# Adapted directly from profiles/pearos/packages.list — see that file's
# own comments for the real bugs already found and fixed in the shared
# non-DE-specific parts (btrfs-progs/cracklib-dicts/squashfs-tools for
# Calamares, mesa-vulkan-drivers/microcode_ctl for AMD+Intel GPU/CPU
# support, grub2/shim for boot). Only the desktop-environment-specific
# packages below actually changed.
# ==============================================================================

# --- Core system ---
fedora-release
dnf
systemd
systemd-udev
systemd-pam
NetworkManager
# Real bug found via research the same night investigating the
# "unmanaged" WiFi device symptom: Fedora splits NetworkManager's
# actual per-medium device-management code into separate plugin
# subpackages (NetworkManager-wifi, NetworkManager-wwan, etc.) --
# confirmed via `dnf repoquery NetworkManager-*` that NetworkManager-
# wifi is a real, distinct package, and this profile's list only had
# NetworkManager-adsl (further down, likely copied from the KDE
# profile without checking relevance). Without NetworkManager-wifi,
# NetworkManager has no WiFi device-management code loaded at all --
# this is a more fundamental gap than wpa_supplicant (below): a device
# would show "unmanaged" regardless of whether a supplicant backend is
# present, since NM never gets far enough to need one.
NetworkManager-wifi
# `iw` (modern wireless config/diagnostic tool) and
# NetworkManager-config-connectivity-fedora (default connectivity-
# check endpoint, needed for the network icon to correctly show
# "connected" vs "connected, no internet") were both missing too --
# neither is a hard dependency of NetworkManager or gnome-shell,
# confirmed present on a full Fedora Workstation install but absent
# from this minimal profile.
iw
NetworkManager-config-connectivity-fedora
# Real bug found via a real installed system on actual hardware
# (2026-09-24): WiFi hardware/driver/firmware all loaded correctly
# (confirmed via dmesg -- mt7921e initializes cleanly, wlp15s0 gets
# created) but `nmcli device status` showed it as "unmanaged" and
# `nmcli device wifi list`/rescan failed with "Error: No Wi-Fi device
# found." NetworkManager cannot actually control a WiFi device without
# a supplicant backend (wpa_supplicant, or iwd) -- confirmed via `dnf
# repoquery --requires NetworkManager` that this is NOT a hard package
# dependency (so a minimal installroot bootstrap never pulls it in),
# even though it's required at runtime for WiFi to function at all.
wpa_supplicant
# Real bug found the same night: this ISO was missing several basic
# diagnostic tools (`lspci`/`ethtool`/`ip link` worked but `ethtool`
# and `lspci` themselves were both "command not found"), which made
# debugging real hardware issues over a live session significantly
# harder than it needed to be. Adding as standard tooling for a
# daily-driver distro, not just for this specific investigation.
ethtool
pciutils
# openssh-server is not installed/enabled by default on this
# Workstation-style spin. Included so users can enable remote access
# for troubleshooting without needing a full package download cycle.
openssh-server
# Real bug found via research the same night, after wpa_supplicant
# alone did not resolve a WiFi device still showing "unmanaged":
# wireless-regdb provides /usr/lib/firmware/regulatory.db, which
# cfg80211 (the kernel's generic wireless layer, sitting below the
# mt7921e driver) needs to set a regulatory domain. Without it, dmesg
# shows "cfg80211: failed to load regulatory.db" and the wifi device
# can be left in a state NetworkManager won't fully manage, even with
# a working supplicant installed. Well-documented issue across
# multiple distros or minimal installs that skip this package. Cheap,
# safe to include regardless of whether it's the full explanation for
# this specific board.
wireless-regdb
kernel-core
kernel-modules
kernel-modules-extra
linux-firmware
# Real bug found via user report on real AMD hardware (2026-09-23, RX
# 6750, still stuck at 800x600 even after mesa-dri-drivers was added):
# Fedora split linux-firmware into per-vendor subpackages, and the
# plain "linux-firmware" metapackage does NOT pull in
# linux-firmware-amdgpu (confirmed via `dnf repoquery --requires
# linux-firmware` on a real Fedora 44 system -- no amdgpu dependency,
# and `rpm -q linux-firmware-amdgpu` shows it's a real, separate,
# not-installed-by-default package). Without the actual GPU firmware
# blobs, amdgpu can't complete real KMS initialization on this
# RDNA2/Navi22 card, so the kernel falls back to the generic EFI
# framebuffer console resolution instead of the monitor's real native
# EDID mode -- mesa-dri-drivers (userspace OpenGL) was necessary but
# not sufficient; this is the actual kernel-side gap.
#
# CORRECTION: the real Fedora 44 package name is "amd-gpu-firmware",
# not "linux-firmware-amdgpu" -- confirmed via `dnf provides
# '/usr/lib/firmware/amdgpu/*'` after the first guess broke the build
# outright ("No match for argument: linux-firmware-amdgpu").
amd-gpu-firmware
# Real bug found via a real installed system on actual hardware
# (2026-09-24): a Realtek RTL8125 (2.5GbE) wired NIC and a MediaTek
# MT7922 WiFi/Bluetooth combo card both showed hardware present but
# non-functional (wired: link negotiated fine but DHCP never
# completed; Bluetooth: dmesg flooded with "Direct firmware load for
# mediatek/BT_RAM_CODE_MT7922_1_1_hdr.bin failed with error -2" every
# ~0.5s, forever). Confirmed via `rpm -qf` on a real Fedora 44 system
# that the RTL8125 firmware ships inside the base linux-firmware
# package (already listed above, so NOT the cause of the wired NIC
# issue -- that one remains unexplained and still needs real
# lspci/dmesg data from a live network to diagnose properly), but the
# MT7922 Bluetooth/WiFi firmware lives in a separate "mt7xxx-firmware"
# subpackage that linux-firmware's own dependencies do NOT pull in --
# same per-vendor-subpackage split pattern as amd-gpu-firmware above.
mt7xxx-firmware
# Real gap found via a live macOS reference comparison 2026-09-25,
# investigating why no Bluetooth status icon ever appears: the
# MT7922 combo card's Bluetooth radio is real and working at the
# kernel level (confirmed live via `rfkill list` -- a genuine
# `hci0: Bluetooth` device, not soft/hard blocked, firmware loading
# cleanly since mt7xxx-firmware was added above), but nothing in this
# profile ever installs the actual Bluetooth stack -- no `bluez`
# daemon, no GNOME integration library -- so the hardware has never
# been usable at all, independent of whether any status icon shows
# for it. Added both; confirmed live (`systemctl enable --now
# bluetooth` -> active). Separately confirmed via GNOME Shell's own
# real source (js/ui/status/bluetooth.js) that the top-bar icon only
# ever appears once a device is actively connected
# (`this._indicator.visible = nConnectedDevices > 0`, hardcoded, no
# gsettings override) -- real macOS shows a permanent Bluetooth icon
# regardless of connection state, a genuine GNOME-vs-macOS design
# difference, not a bug this package addition fixes by itself. See
# docs/gnome-phase1-findings.md for the full trace.
bluez
gnome-bluetooth
# Real bug found via a real installed system on actual hardware
# (2026-09-24): GNOME Terminal and Nautilus (Files) both failed to
# open -- Terminal showed an endless loading-cursor spinner with no
# window ever appearing, Files silently did nothing. Root-caused via
# the installed system's own journal (mounted read-only from the
# hypervisor via qemu-nbd, read offline): gnome-terminal-server exits
# immediately with "Locale not supported." (status 9) because
# en_US.UTF-8 -- the locale Calamares' localecfg module writes to
# /etc/locale.conf based on the Welcome page's language selection --
# was never actually generated on this system; Fedora ships locale
# data split per-language into glibc-langpack-<lang> RPMs rather than
# generating it on demand, and none were ever installed. This also
# explains the "-bash: warning: setlocale: LC_NUMERIC/LC_TIME: cannot
# change locale (en_US.UTF-8): No such file or directory" warnings
# seen on every single login all night, and is the most likely cause
# of Nautilus's repeated ABRT crashes (dbus-:1.2-org.gnome.Nautilus
# service exiting with status=6/core-dump) seen in the same journal
# window, though that failure mode wasn't directly isolated to the
# locale as precisely as Terminal's was.
glibc-langpack-en
dracut
dracut-live

# --- Hardware support (AMD + Intel, see pearos profile's own comment
# for the real gaps this was found to close) ---
# Real bug found on real AMD hardware (2026-09-23, RX 6750): the live
# session rendered at a horribly low resolution on a real 1080p
# monitor. mesa-vulkan-drivers alone is Vulkan-only -- mesa-dri-drivers
# (radeonsi/iris, the actual OpenGL/GLX driver Mutter needs for
# hardware-accelerated rendering and correct native-EDID resolution
# under both X11 and Wayland) was never listed at all, so Mutter almost
# certainly fell back to software rendering (llvmpipe) or a degraded
# mode instead of the real amdgpu/radeonsi path.
mesa-dri-drivers
mesa-vulkan-drivers
microcode_ctl
# Real bug found via a real disk-install test on actual hardware
# (2026-09-23): parchaos-gnome-calamares-config's parchaos-install-kernel
# script populates the installed system's ESP from grub2-efi-x64's own
# "master" files under /usr/lib/efi/grub2/*/EFI/fedora/ (grubx64.efi,
# grub.cfg) -- grub2-efi-x64-cdboot (below) only provides gcdx64.efi and
# depends on grub2-common, NOT grub2-efi-x64 itself (confirmed via `dnf
# repoquery --requires grub2-efi-x64-cdboot`), so those master files were
# never actually present. Non-fatal on its own (the script only warns:
# "ESP not populated, UEFI boot will not work") but a real gap toward a
# working installed system. Also present, latent, in the KDE (pearos)
# profile's own packages.list -- not yet confirmed whether it's been hit
# there too.
grub2-efi-x64
grub2-pc-modules
grub2-efi-x64-cdboot
grub2-efi-x64-modules
shim-x64
mokutil
sudo
passwd
util-linux
procps-ng
bash-completion
polkit
dbus-broker

# --- Live-media specifics ---
livesys-scripts
plymouth
plymouth-theme-spinner

# Installer (Calamares, same as the KDE profile — Fedora ships it
# directly, no COPR/build needed).
calamares

# Calamares partition/users/unpackfs module dependencies — same three
# real bugs already found/fixed once in the KDE profile (see its
# packages.list comments for the exact failure modes): mkfs.btrfs,
# password dictionary validation, and unsquashfs all need explicit
# packages Fedora doesn't pull in by default.
btrfs-progs
cracklib-dicts
squashfs-tools

# --- GNOME desktop ---
# GNOME Shell is Wayland-native. Real bug found via a real dnf query
# (2026-09-22): unlike the KDE profile's kwin-x11 fallback, Fedora 44 no
# longer ships a separate "gnome-session-xsession" Xorg-fallback
# subpackage at all (confirmed via `dnf repoquery '*gnome-session*'` —
# only gnome-session and gnome-session-wayland-session exist) — dropped
# rather than guessing at a different name; GNOME on Xorg isn't a
# priority for this project anyway (Wayland-only, same as the KDE
# profile's own real-world default).
gnome-shell
gnome-session
gnome-session-wayland-session
gnome-control-center
gdm
xdg-desktop-portal-gnome
xdg-desktop-portal
gnome-keyring
# Real bug avoided (2026-09-23): not confirmed as a transitive
# dependency of gnome-shell via `dnf repoquery --requires` -- adding
# explicitly since customize.sh's dconf-based extension-enablement
# mechanism needs the `dconf` CLI at build time (Phase 5.5).
dconf

# Real, confirmed Fedora package (`dnf repoquery gnome-shell-extension-user-theme`)
# needed to apply a GNOME Shell-level theme (panel/overview chrome) --
# distinct from org.gnome.desktop.interface's gtk-theme key, which only
# affects GTK app chrome, not the Shell itself. Needed for
# parchaos-gtk-theme's Shell theme to actually apply.
gnome-shell-extension-user-theme

gnome-shell-extension-common
gnome-disk-utility
gnome-system-monitor
gnome-firmware
NetworkManager-adsl

# --- Apps referenced by Pulsar OS's / pearOS's default app set (Fedora
# equivalents for now — Pulsar OS's own forks like the patched Nautilus
# and Sayri come from this profile's own COPR once ported, see
# packages.sh, same pattern as pafari/pearos-dock in the KDE profile) ---
# nautilus deliberately NOT listed here (2026-09-23) -- parchaos-finder
# (Parcher, packages.sh) Obsoletes/Conflicts/Provides it directly, same
# drop-in-replacement pattern as the real Pulsar OS package itself.
gnome-terminal
gnome-text-editor
loupe
file-roller
gnome-screenshot
gnome-calculator
gnome-weather
gnome-calendar
gnome-contacts

# AirDrop/Handoff equivalent — GSConnect, a real GNOME Shell extension
# (confirmed listed in Pulsar OS's own bundled-extensions table,
# andyholmes/gnome-shell-extension-gsconnect upstream). Fedora ships it
# directly.
gnome-shell-extension-gsconnect

# macOS-style trackpad gestures — same real Fedora package the KDE
# profile uses (touchegg, DE-agnostic system-level daemon); config
# still needs porting/verifying for GNOME's own gesture conventions.
touchegg

# --- Fonts / basics needed for the branding layer to render correctly ---
google-noto-sans-fonts
google-noto-emoji-fonts
# Real bug found via a real installed system on actual hardware
# (2026-09-24): user reported "strange letter spacing" in the Terminal
# app. Root-caused live on a fresh install: `gsettings get
# org.gnome.desktop.interface font-name` returns "Adwaita Sans 11" and
# the terminal/monospace default is "Adwaita Mono" (both GNOME's own
# current-generation system font family), but neither font was ever
# actually installed -- `fc-match 'Adwaita Mono'` fell back to "Noto
# Sans" (a PROPORTIONAL font substituted for a monospace request,
# which is what produced the visibly wrong, uneven letter spacing in
# VTE's fixed-width cell layout), and `fc-match 'Adwaita Sans'` fell
# back to "Cantarell" -- meaning every window title, menu, and label
# across the ENTIRE desktop was silently rendering in the wrong font,
# not just the terminal. Confirmed via `dnf provides` on a real
# Fedora 44 system that these are real, separate packages that were
# simply never added to this list.
adwaita-sans-fonts
adwaita-mono-fonts

# Real bug found via the first real branded build attempt (2026-09-23):
# customize.sh's flathub remote-add step failed with "execv(flatpak)
# failed: No such file or directory" -- flatpak was never added to
# this profile's own package list at all (present in the KDE profile's
# packages.list, missed when adapting it for GNOME).
flatpak

# --- Broad hardware/media support + standard utilities ---
# Real gap found via research the same night as the WiFi
# investigation (2026-09-24): this profile's package list is hand-
# curated rather than built from Fedora's own comps.xml groups (a
# deliberate choice, see this file's header -- switching to the full
# official spin bundle now would roughly double image size and
# conflict with branding already built against this minimal base).
# But diffing against comps.xml's own "standard" and "hardware-
# support" groups (`dnf group info standard hardware-support` on a
# real Fedora system) surfaced real, relevant gaps beyond just the
# WiFi ones already fixed above -- most notably filesystem drivers for
# removable media (exfatprogs/ntfs-3g/dosfstools), which likely
# explains part of tonight's earlier USB-drive mounting trouble, and
# firmware for WiFi/audio/GPU vendors OTHER than this specific
# machine's AMD/MediaTek/Realtek hardware -- relevant for this being a
# general-purpose daily-driver distro, not just this one board.
# Server/enterprise-oriented standard-group packages (sssd-proxy,
# realmd, nfs-utils, quota, crontabs, mcelog, sos) deliberately
# excluded as not relevant to a desktop/gaming rig.
usbutils
# Real bug found on the real machine this was all debugged on tonight
# (2026-09-24): offline all evening (the whole point of tonight's
# crisis), its clock drifted ~5 hours behind real time with nothing to
# correct it once network finally came up. That stale clock then made
# rpm reject a freshly COPR-signed package's signature as "not live
# yet" (signature validity is time-window-based) -- looked exactly
# like a GPG trust/corruption problem at first, wasted real
# troubleshooting time before the actual cause (no NTP client at all)
# was found. `chrony` was on Fedora's own "standard" comps group (see
# the block below) but got missed in that curation pass -- important
# enough on its own, not just a subset of that group, to call out
# separately here. Every future device that's offline for a while
# before first getting network will hit this same failure without it.
chrony
net-tools
dosfstools
exfatprogs
ntfs-3g
ntfsprogs
tcpdump
mtr
traceroute
nmap-ncat
rsync
unzip
zip
tree
lsof
smartmontools
wget2-wget
# Firmware for hardware other than this specific machine's own
# AMD/MediaTek/Realtek combo -- Intel/Atheros/Broadcom/Marvell WiFi,
# Cirrus/Intel audio, Intel/NVIDIA GPU -- so this ISO works reasonably
# well on other real hardware too, not just the board this was
# debugged on tonight.
alsa-sof-firmware
alsa-firmware
atheros-firmware
brcmfmac-firmware
cirrus-audio-firmware
intel-audio-firmware
intel-gpu-firmware
iwlegacy-firmware
iwlwifi-dvm-firmware
iwlwifi-mvm-firmware
libertas-firmware
nvidia-gpu-firmware
nxpwireless-firmware
realtek-firmware
tiwilink-firmware
usb_modeswitch
# Real gap found 2026-09-24: parcha-dock and every other GNOME Shell
# extension this profile ships ARE enabled by default (see
# customize.sh's dconf enabled-extensions default), but modern GNOME
# (45+) has no built-in way to open an extension's preferences dialog
# -- that's exclusively the job of the official Extensions app
# (org.gnome.Extensions). Without it there was no gear icon, no menu
# entry, nothing: a working extension's real settings (dock size,
# position, autohide, magnification, etc.) were completely
# unreachable from the desktop. `gnome-extensions prefs <uuid>` from a
# terminal always worked, which is how this got missed until a real
# user asked "where are the dock's options."
gnome-extensions-app
# Chromium/Blink engine for parchaos-browser (packages.sh). Confirmed
# via real `dnf list --available` on real hardware 2026-09-24:
# `chromium-freeworld` (RPM Fusion) doesn't exist for this Fedora
# release -- this is Fedora's own plain `chromium`, which has shipped
# with Google API keys already stripped since 2021 (no baked-in
# Sign-in/Sync nagging). H.264 support comes from the
# fedora-cisco-openh264 repo this profile already enables.
chromium
# Mail.app equivalent -- confirmed via real `dnf repoquery -l geary` on
# real hardware that this ships org.gnome.Geary.desktop (renamed to
# "Mail" by parchaos-app-renames, packages.sh).
geary
# Clock.app equivalent -- renamed to "Clock" by parchaos-app-renames.
gnome-clocks

# Real macOS-polish extensions, matching Pulsar OS's own real config
# (see customize.sh's 00-parchaos-extensions comment for the full
# reasoning) -- confirmed via real `dnf list --available` these are
# genuine official Fedora packages with the same UUIDs Pulsar OS uses.
gnome-shell-extension-blur-my-shell
gnome-shell-extension-just-perfection
gnome-shell-extension-no-overview
gnome-shell-extension-appindicator

# GUI update tool -- covers this need with zero packaging work at all,
# confirmed via real `dnf list --available` this is a genuine official
# Fedora package (50.4-1.fc44). Same real job as Pulsar OS's own
# pulsaros-update (blocked, no LICENSE file), without needing any of
# their code.
gnome-software
# Real runtime dependency for parchaos-hanabi (packages.sh) -- Hanabi's
# own README documents `gtk4paintablesink` (this package) as its
# default video sink for the live-wallpaper renderer. Confirmed as a
# real Fedora package via `dnf list --available` on real hardware.
gstreamer1-plugin-gtk4
